  "order": 24,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "vxo0vjv",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Properties of Carbon"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Carbon exhibits tetravalency and unique catenation ability, allowing it to form a vast variety of stable organic compounds.",
        "Allotropes of carbon present different physical structures: Diamond is exceptionally hard and non-conducting, while Graphite is soft, lubricant-like, and conducts electricity.",
        "Hydrocarbons are classified into Saturated (alkanes with single C-C bonds) and Unsaturated (alkenes and alkynes containing double or triple bonds).",
        "Functional groups such as alcohols (-OH), aldehydes (-CHO), ketones (>C=O), and carboxylic acids (-COOH) dictate the chemical properties of carbon compounds.",
        "Soaps form spherical structures called micelles in water to trap hydrophobic dirt, whereas synthetic detergents remain effective even in hard water."
      ],
      "id": "sdmtgpb"
    },
